-- Passage rg8-figurative-language-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6bd74f3b-abac-5678-803e-68351084f0c7', 'rg8-figurative-language-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Nature's Engineers$q$, $q$Few animals change a landscape as much as the North American beaver. Wildlife scientists often call beavers nature's engineers, and the title fits. A single family can turn a quiet stream into a wide pond in one season.

A beaver's front teeth are its toolbox. They never stop growing, and they are coated with a hard orange layer that keeps them sharp. With them, a beaver can cut through a small tree in a single night. The animal then drags branches to the water and weaves them into a dam, packing the gaps with mud and stones.

The dam slows the current, and the water behind it spreads out. That new pond becomes a nursery for fish, frogs, and insects. Ducks and herons arrive to feed. Even the soil benefits, because the slow water drops rich sediment along the banks.

Not everyone welcomes these busy builders. When a dam floods a road or a farm field, some landowners describe beavers as pests. Others prefer a gentler label and call them stubborn neighbors.

Today, some communities try to share the land rather than remove the animals. Workers install pipes through dams so that water drains at a safe level. The beavers keep working, the roads stay dry, and the wetland keeps growing. In many places, the phrase "busy as a beaver" now sounds less like a complaint and more like praise.$q$, 'draft')
