-- Passage rg8-figurative-language-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2d7f08c4-8dab-54c2-871c-7a3e1b97b79f', 'rg8-figurative-language-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Captain's Rulebook$q$, $q$Kenji had been named captain of the Ridgeview Robotics Club, and he took the job seriously. Maybe too seriously. On the first day, he handed every member a twelve-page rulebook.

Faith flipped through the pages and found a rule about how to fold lunch napkins. "Wow," she said. "You really thought of everything." A few members snickered.

The team's budget was tiny, so Kenji insisted on using cardboard and rubber bands for the first prototype. Some members grumbled that he was being cheap. Faith defended him. "He's not cheap," she said. "He's thrifty. There's a difference."

The prototype was a disaster. Its cardboard arm drooped like a wilted flower. The robot rolled in slow circles, bumped through the half-open door, and wandered off into the hallway. The room went quiet.

Then Kenji started laughing. "Well," he said, "at least it knows how to make an exit."

Everyone laughed with him, and the tension in the room melted away. Over the next two weeks, the team rebuilt the robot with a lighter frame and better wheels. Kenji even let Faith rewrite the rulebook. Her version was one page long.

At the regional competition, the robot finished third out of twenty teams. Kenji's careful plans had kept the team organized all season. On the bus ride home, Faith handed him a trophy made from a cardboard tube and a rubber band.

"For the captain who really thought of everything," she said. This time, she meant every word.$q$, 'draft')
