-- Passage rg8-figurative-language-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b50ed876-622f-562a-abf8-d73bb0d6af55', 'rg8-figurative-language-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why Headlines Love Puns$q$, $q$Walk past any school bulletin board or flip through a community newsletter, and you will probably spot a pun. A pun is a play on words. It uses a word with two meanings, or two words that sound alike, to make a reader think twice.

Headline writers are especially fond of puns. Imagine a story about a bakery that won a regional award. A writer might title it "Local Bakery Rises to the Occasion." Bread rises in the oven, but a person who rises to the occasion meets a challenge successfully. The single word carries both ideas at once.

Puns work because English is full of words with several meanings. The word "bat" can name a flying mammal or a piece of sports equipment. The word "current" can mean a flow of water or electricity, or it can mean up to date. An article about a science club's electricity project might be titled "Students Stay Current."

Not every reader enjoys wordplay. Some people groan at puns and call them cheap tricks. Others admire them as tiny puzzles that reward careful attention. Teachers often use puns to show students that reading is an active process. To get the joke, you have to notice that a word is doing double duty.

The next time a headline makes you roll your eyes, read it again. Behind the groan, there may be a small piece of clever craftsmanship.$q$, 'draft')
