-- Passage rg8-figurative-language-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f94e5168-e149-5020-a7a6-b2ff9676ff2b', 'rg8-figurative-language-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Room 114$q$, $q$Ms. Okonkwo's debate team met in Room 114 every Tuesday, and the first rule was simple: arrive prepared. This Tuesday, Rohan arrived with a granola bar and no notes.

"Ah, Rohan," Ms. Okonkwo said, glancing at his empty hands. "I see you've brought your usual mountain of research."

Rohan sank into his chair. Lucia, who sat beside him, had three color-coded folders and a stack of index cards.

The practice topic was whether the school day should start thirty minutes later. Lucia argued that teenagers need more sleep. Her points were clear and well organized, and the judge's sheet called her "articulate." Rohan read the comment over her shoulder. "In my last debate," he whispered, "they called me chatty."

"Same idea," Lucia whispered back. "Different flavor."

Rohan was arguing against the later start. When his turn came, he stood up slowly and yawned. "I'll admit I'm not fully awake," he said, "which is pretty strong evidence for my opponent's side." The room burst into laughter, and even Ms. Okonkwo smiled.

Then Rohan surprised everyone. He argued that a later start would push after-school sports into the dark evening hours. He used facts he had memorized from a newspaper article at breakfast. His points landed one after another.

Afterward, Ms. Okonkwo stopped him at the door. "Next week," she said, "bring the notes too. A mind like yours deserves a paper trail."$q$, 'draft')
