-- Passage rg8-figurative-language-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fdde9a57-60d5-59d2-92d7-776db8e6d3c5', 'rg8-figurative-language-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Saving the Clock Tower$q$, $q$For more than a century, the clock tower on the town square of Fairhaven Falls has kept watch over its streets. Built in 1898, the brick tower once told farmers when to bring their wagons to market. It also told students when to hurry to class.

By the 1990s, though, the clock had stopped. Its hands froze at twenty minutes past four, and pigeons moved into the silent gears. Some residents called the tower outdated and wanted it torn down to make room for a parking lot. Others called it historic and argued that it was the heart of the town.

A retired machinist named Walter Abiodun decided to settle the question with his own hands. He spent two winters cleaning rusted gears and carving replacement parts. Students from the high school shop class joined him on weekends. The local historical society raised money with a pancake breakfast and a sign that read: "Help us make time for the tower."

On a bright morning in May, the clock chimed for the first time in nineteen years. People came out of shops and stood on the sidewalks to listen. One shop owner said the sound was like an old friend calling her name.

Today the clock keeps accurate time again. Visitors sometimes ask why the town worked so hard to save something so old. Residents usually point to the tower and give a simple answer. Some things are worth more than the time they tell.$q$, 'draft')
