-- Passage rg8-literary-structure-pov-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5e04c179-a9a8-5a40-9c61-0e23e6174a3f', 'rg8-literary-structure-pov-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Third Place$q$, $q$The judge pinned a yellow ribbon to Nadia's poster, and the gym filled with polite applause. Third place. Nadia smiled for the photo, but her eyes went to the corner of her display, where a lumpy plastic bottle sat full of gravel, sand and charcoal.

Six weeks earlier, that bottle had been a disaster.

Her first water filter leaked from the bottom and soaked the kitchen counter. Her second filter worked so slowly that she fell asleep waiting for one cup of water to drip through. Her third filter was fast, but the water came out grayer than it went in. Her little brother, Felix, held the cup up to the light. "Congratulations," he said. "You invented dirt soup."

Nadia almost quit that night. She wrote PROJECT CANCELED on a sticky note and stuck it to the bottle. In the morning, she found a second sticky note under hers, in Felix's messy handwriting: Try washing the charcoal first?

It was such a simple idea that she was annoyed she had not thought of it. She rinsed the charcoal until the water ran clear, rebuilt the layers, and poured in a cup of muddy creek water. Twenty minutes later, a clear stream dripped into the jar.

Now, in the gym, a younger student stopped in front of her display. "Did it work the first time?" he asked.

Nadia peeled two faded sticky notes off the back of her poster and handed them to him. "Not even close," she said.$q$, 'draft')
