-- Passage rg8-main-idea-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1bbb76c8-c8c1-560b-a8f2-7212f530f898', 'rg8-main-idea-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why Cities Run Hot$q$, $q$On a summer afternoon, a downtown street can be several degrees warmer than a field just outside the city. Scientists call this pattern an urban heat island. It happens because cities change the surfaces that sunlight lands on, and those surfaces store and release heat differently than plants and soil do.

Dark materials are a big part of the problem. Asphalt roads and tar roofs absorb most of the sunlight that hits them instead of reflecting it. They warm up during the day and then slowly give off that heat at night, so city neighborhoods often stay warm long after sunset.

Cities also lose a natural cooling system when trees and grass are removed. Plants pull water from the ground and release it through their leaves. As that water evaporates, it carries heat away from the air, much like sweat cools a runner. A parking lot cannot do this.

Buildings add to the effect as well. Tall walls trap warm air between them and block breezes that might carry it away. Air conditioners, cars and machines release extra heat directly into the streets.

The good news is that the same knowledge points toward solutions. Some towns now paint roofs white or light gray so they reflect sunlight. Others plant trees along sidewalks and build small parks on empty lots. In one study of a midsize city, blocks with more shade trees stayed noticeably cooler during heat waves than nearby blocks without them. Understanding why cities trap heat helps planners design neighborhoods that stay safer and more comfortable.$q$, 'draft')
