-- Passage rg8-main-idea-summary-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c4166e12-f7da-5d4e-9707-3948244136e6', 'rg8-main-idea-summary-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$When Every Town Had Its Own Time$q$, $q$For most of history, people set their clocks by the sun. When the sun reached its highest point in the sky, it was noon. Because the sun moves across the sky from east to west, noon arrived a few minutes earlier in an eastern town than in a town farther west. Each community kept its own local time, and for a long while this caused few problems.

Railroads changed that. A train could travel between dozens of towns in a single day, and each town might show a slightly different time on its station clock. Railroad companies had to print timetables that listed departures in many local times at once. Passengers grew confused, and workers struggled to keep trains on schedule. A traveler who misread a timetable could miss a connection by several minutes.

To solve the problem, railroad companies began to divide the country into a small number of time zones. Inside each zone, every clock would show the same time, even if it did not match the sun exactly. Towns near the edge of a zone had to accept that noon on their clocks no longer lined up with the sun overhead.

Not everyone welcomed the change at first. Some townspeople felt that the railroads had no right to decide what time it was. Over the following years, however, schools, banks and businesses found that shared time made planning easier. Eventually, governments made time zones official.

Today, most people never think about who decided what time it is. Yet the clocks on our phones still follow a system that grew out of the need to run trains safely and on time.$q$, 'draft')
