-- Passage rg8-main-idea-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('961458f5-41fb-5fb6-bf4c-012a28403439', 'rg8-main-idea-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How Your Map Knows Where You Are$q$, $q$When you open a map on a phone, a small dot shows exactly where you are standing. That dot depends on a system of satellites circling high above Earth. Understanding how the system works reveals that finding a location is really a problem of measuring time.

Each satellite carries an extremely accurate clock. It constantly sends out a signal that says, in effect, which satellite it is and exactly when the signal left. A receiver in a phone picks up the signal and notes when it arrived. Because radio signals travel at a known speed, the tiny delay tells the receiver how far away that satellite is.

One distance is not enough. Knowing you are a certain distance from one satellite only places you somewhere on a huge imaginary sphere. With signals from several satellites, the receiver can find the single point where all those distances agree. Receivers usually need at least four satellites to pin down a location and correct for errors in the phone's own clock.

Timing has to be extraordinarily precise. Signals travel so fast that an error of one millionth of a second could throw the location off by hundreds of meters. That is why the satellites' clocks are so carefully built and checked.

The system is not perfect. Tall buildings can block or bounce signals, and a phone indoors may struggle to find enough satellites. To help, phones often combine satellite data with information from nearby cell towers and wireless networks. Still, the basic idea remains the same. Every time the dot moves on your map, it is the result of a fast and careful calculation of time and distance.$q$, 'draft')
