-- Passage rg8-text-structure-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0d07d012-a827-5220-bc8c-10f475bfffc9', 'rg8-text-structure-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Bridge on Mill Creek$q$, $q$In the 1880s, the farming town of Brenfield sat on both sides of Mill Creek. Only the west bank had the general store, the school, and the train station. Families on the east bank crossed by ferry, a flat wooden raft pulled along a rope. In spring, when melting snow swelled the creek, the ferry often could not run for weeks.

The problem grew worse each year. Children on the east bank missed school, and farmers could not get their crops to the train. In 1887, a mill owner named Tobias Wendell and a schoolteacher named Rosa Delgado called a town meeting to discuss a bridge. Many residents liked the idea, but the town had very little money.

The two organizers came up with a plan. Instead of paying workers, each family would give either labor or materials. Farmers hauled stone from their fields with ox teams. The mill cut lumber for free during the winter, when business was slow. Students collected nails and sold baked goods to buy iron bolts.

Construction began in the summer of 1888, when the creek was at its lowest. Workers first built two stone piers in the water. Next, they laid heavy beams across the piers. Finally, they added a plank deck and railings. The bridge opened in October, just before the first frost.

The bridge changed daily life in Brenfield. Attendance at the school rose, and east bank farmers shipped their harvest on time. Today a newer bridge stands in the same spot, but the original stone piers still rise from the water beside it.$q$, 'draft')
