insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e354c65f-c33c-54dc-998c-c034906bfbfc', 'rg8-text-structure-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Saving the Sunflower Mural$q$, $q$For thirty years, a mural of sunflowers and bicycles covered the side wall of the Eastgate Community Center. Painted by neighborhood teenagers in the 1990s, it had become a local landmark. By last spring, though, the paint was peeling in long strips, and whole petals had faded to gray.

The cause was not hard to find. The wall faced south, so it baked in direct sun every afternoon. Rainwater also leaked from a broken gutter and soaked into the bricks behind the paint. When the wet bricks froze in winter, the paint cracked and lifted.

A group of residents led by a retired art teacher, Ines Takahashi, decided to restore the mural instead of painting over it. They tackled the causes before touching the art. First, volunteers repaired the gutter so water would drain away from the wall. Next, they scraped off loose paint and sealed the bricks. Only then did artists repaint the faded sections, matching the original colors from old photographs.

To prevent the same damage in the future, the group chose paint made to resist sunlight and added a clear protective coat on top. They also planned a yearly check of the wall each fall.

In September, the community celebrated the restored mural with a block party. Several of the original painters, now adults, came back to see their sunflowers bright again.$q$, 'draft')
