-- Passage rg8-theme-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3e8b2bb6-864c-5f74-852b-710d7e8e2ff6', 'rg8-theme-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-theme-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Gear That Would Not Turn$q$, $q$Mateo had built most of the robot himself, and he liked to say so. When the Eastfield Middle School team met in the science lab after school, he sat closest to the machine and kept the screwdriver in his pocket. Aiyana wrote the code, Kwame kept the parts list, and Lin designed the arm, but Mateo made the final changes. He trusted his own hands more than anyone else's.

Two days before the regional contest, the arm stopped lifting. Mateo took it apart and put it back together three times. He stayed in the lab until the custodian flicked the lights. The gear still slipped every time the arm reached halfway up.

The next afternoon, Lin watched him for a while without saying anything. Then she pulled her notebook from her bag and opened it to a drawing she had made in October. "The gear I designed had twelve teeth," she said quietly. "That one has ten."

Mateo stared at the part. He had swapped it weeks ago because the smaller gear fit more easily, and he had never mentioned it to anyone.

Kwame found a twelve-tooth gear in the parts bin in less than a minute, because he had labeled every drawer. Aiyana adjusted one line of code so the arm would move more slowly. By five o'clock, the arm lifted the test block smoothly to the top.

At the contest, the judges asked who had built the robot. Mateo started to answer, then stopped. "All of us," he said, and he stepped back so Lin could explain the arm.$q$, 'draft')
