insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('668192ac-b662-5c18-b989-30b16d3dfe49', 'rg9-argument-evidence-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Instruments on the Library Shelves$q$, $q$The Dunmore Public Library already lends books, movies and board games. It should also lend musical instruments, starting with guitars, keyboards and violins, so that any family in town can try music without spending hundreds of dollars.

Cost is the biggest barrier. A beginner violin at the music shop on Alder Street costs about two hundred dollars, and many children quit within a year. A library loan would let a family find out whether a child truly enjoys playing before they buy anything.

The idea has worked elsewhere. The library in Corrin Falls began lending twenty instruments three years ago. According to its annual report, the instruments were checked out more than four hundred times last year, and the waiting list is usually over a month long.

Demand exists here, too. Our school's band director, Ms. Okafor, says that every fall several students ask to join the band but cannot get an instrument in time for the first rehearsal.

Some people worry that borrowed instruments will be damaged. Corrin Falls handles this by asking borrowers to sign a care agreement and by having a volunteer check each instrument when it is returned. Its librarians report that only three instruments needed repairs last year.

Finally, music makes people smarter. My cousin started piano at age six, and now she is the top student in her class, which proves that every child who borrows an instrument will do better in school.

A library exists to open doors. An instrument collection would open one of the most rewarding doors of all.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
