-- Passage rg9-argument-evidence-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('db35ec01-9983-543a-bc87-aa8201eaffdf', 'rg9-argument-evidence-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Give Ultimate a Chance$q$, $q$Stillwater High offers eleven sports, but many students do not play any of them. The athletic department should add ultimate, the team sport played with a flying disc, as a spring club sport next year.

Ultimate is inexpensive to start. A team needs a field, a set of cones and a few discs, which cost about fifteen dollars each. Compare that with the thousands of dollars our school spends each year on football helmets and pads.

The sport also welcomes beginners. Players cannot run while holding the disc, so the game depends on passing and teamwork rather than on one star athlete. Students who have never played an organized sport can learn the basic rules in a single afternoon.

Interest is strong. Last month, the student council posted a sign-up sheet in the cafeteria asking who would join an ultimate team. Sixty-two students signed it, which is more than enough for two full teams.

Ultimate also teaches fairness. In most games, there are no referees. Players call their own fouls and settle disagreements by talking them through, which gives students practice in honesty and respect.

Some coaches worry that a new sport would pull athletes away from track and baseball, which also play in spring. However, a follow-up survey of the sixty-two students who signed up found that only nine already play a spring sport. Most would be new to school athletics.

Adding ultimate would cost little and reach students who are not being reached now. The school should give it a chance.$q$, 'draft')
