   $q$What did the Brayford study actually find?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Playing video games for three hours a week improves hand control.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"},{"text":"Surgeons who play video games have fewer problems in real operations.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralization"},{"text":"Trainees who played games did better on a simulator task than others.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Hospitals that use simulators train surgeons faster than others.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Trainees who played games did better on a simulator task than others.$q$, $q$The first paragraph says gamers "finished the task faster and made fewer errors" on the simulator. The study did not measure real operations or show that gaming improved anyone's skills.$q$, 'draft', '583997bb-2bdb-5dbc-9d26-d262feeeb181')
