-- Passage rg9-argument-evidence-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('21e450e6-d9b0-54da-801e-e62e3cdee874', 'rg9-argument-evidence-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$A Report on the Standing Desks$q$, $q$In January, Room 114 at Kesterton High replaced its regular desks with standing desks that can be raised or lowered. At the end of the semester, the teacher, Mr. Adeyemi, wrote a report for the school board asking that every classroom receive the new desks. His report makes several claims, and some are better supported than others.

Mr. Adeyemi's first claim is that students were more active. The desks came with step counters, and the average student in Room 114 took about nine hundred more steps per class period than students in a nearby room with regular desks. This evidence is directly related to the claim, and it was measured rather than guessed.

His second claim is that students focused better. To support it, he notes that the class average on unit tests rose from 78 percent in the fall to 84 percent in the spring. However, the spring units covered different material, and students often improve as they get used to a teacher and a subject. The rise in scores may have nothing to do with the desks.

His third claim is that students liked the desks. He surveyed his class, and 26 of 29 students said they preferred the new desks. This is reasonable evidence for the claim, although some students may simply have enjoyed having something new.

His final claim is that standing desks will improve health across the whole school. For this, he offers no evidence beyond his own classroom, which had one teacher, one subject and twenty-nine students over a single semester.

The board should take the report seriously but carefully. A trial in several classrooms, with different teachers and subjects, would show whether the results in Room 114 hold up.$q$, 'draft')
