   $q$Why does the author begin with the story of the carrot named Gerald?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To show that children are better than adults at choosing vegetables","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralizes"},{"text":"To explain why carrots sometimes split while they are growing","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-purpose"},{"text":"To prove that oddly shaped carrots are more common than straight ones","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"To introduce the problem of wasted produce in a personal, lighthearted way","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To introduce the problem of wasted produce in a personal, lighthearted way$q$, $q$The playful name draws readers in before the author admits that "a carrot like Gerald almost never reaches a grocery store shelf," which leads into the essay's real subject. The essay never explains why carrots split.$q$, 'draft', '35cd9fa5-dae1-53c2-8ac2-408db2fc21f0')
