-- Passage rg9-authors-purpose-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('be7f35b7-5ee5-5432-9979-253699d097c6', 'rg9-authors-purpose-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Look at the Boulder First$q$, $q$From a visitor guide written by Sebastian Okafor, a naturalist at Granite Falls State Park.

Most visitors to Granite Falls come for the waterfall, and I do not blame them. But on your way up the trail, I hope you will stop at the large gray boulder near the second bridge and look closely at its surface. Those crusty patches of pale green, orange, and gray are not stains or dried paint. They are lichens, and they are some of the most remarkable living things in this park.

A lichen is not a single organism. It is a partnership between a fungus and a tiny organism that can make food from sunlight, usually an alga. The fungus builds the structure and holds on to water, and its partner produces sugars that feed them both. Together, they can survive in places where neither could live alone: bare rock, tree bark, even old fence posts.

Lichens grow slowly, sometimes less than a millimeter a year. A patch the size of your hand on that boulder may be older than anyone in your family. Over centuries, lichens help break down rock into the first thin layers of soil, which is why scientists sometimes call them pioneers.

They also work as an early warning system. Many lichens are extremely sensitive to pollution in the air, and they disappear when the air turns dirty. When researchers want to know how clean the air in a forest is, one of the first things they do is count the lichens. The rich variety on our trails is good news.

Please do not scrape or peel them. A careless thumbnail can erase decades of growth in a second. Look with your eyes, or better yet with a magnifying glass, which you can borrow free at the visitor center.

The waterfall will still be there when you arrive. Give the boulder a minute first.$q$, 'draft')
