insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5344bbe8-db9c-50be-8140-c9dadbd13a83', 'rg9-authors-purpose-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$A Note About Mosquitoes$q$, $q$From a notice by Dr. Farida Haddad, director of the Pell County Health Department, mailed to households at the start of summer.

Every summer, our office receives hundreds of calls about mosquitoes. People ask when the county will spray, why their neighborhood seems worse than others, and whether there is anything they can do. The honest answer to the last question is yes, and it may matter more than anything we do.

The mosquitoes that bother most of us around our homes do not come from distant swamps. Many of them hatch within a short flight of where they bite. A female mosquito can lay her eggs in a surprisingly small amount of water: a bottle cap, a clogged gutter, a child's forgotten toy, or the saucer under a flowerpot. In warm weather, those eggs can become flying adults in about a week.

That means the most effective way to reduce mosquitoes is to remove the water before they can use it. Once a week, walk around your yard and tip out anything holding water. Change the water in birdbaths and pet bowls. Clear leaves from gutters, and drill small holes in the bottoms of tire swings and outdoor bins.

Spraying has its place, and the county will continue to do it when testing shows a need. But spraying kills only the adults that are flying at that moment. It does nothing about the next batch already growing in a bucket behind the garage.

Some mosquitoes can carry illnesses, so this is about more than comfort. Still, there is no reason for alarm. Most bites cause nothing worse than an itch, and a few minutes of effort each week can make a real difference on your street.

Our office cannot reach into every backyard. You can. Thank you for being our partners this summer.$q$, 'draft')
